hi there steve kaufman here um today i  want to talk about  what teachers often get wrong  now  uh i know a lot of very good language  teachers  i'm talking to two of them  check tutors via skype  some of our best tutors at link are  teachers  lots of teachers get it right  but i wanted to mention  where i think they get it wrong some of  them maybe many of them i had a comment  from a person called riva  on my most recent post about the meetup  in prague  and she i'm assuming it's a she  said that  she wishes that she had spent more time  listening and reading and developing her  ability to understand czech and to enjoy  the language rather than  doing what she needed to do to pass her  tests  and the teacher for example insisted  that they learn  certain czech proverbs  okay now that's just one example  i am not the slightest bit interested in  proverbs  when i learn a language i don't know  proverbs i don't care about them but  there's nothing wrong with proverbs  if the teacher is interested in proverbs  that's great  but why would the teacher insist that  the student has to know and learn these  proverbs  and then be tested on them  to me that's just plain ridiculous  uh it's far better for the student to  spend his or her time  on  things that are of interest to the  student in fact proverbs are not very  important at all  if we look at the real goals of language  learning which are  comprehension and communication  the same is true with a lot of the  grammar tests that students are given  fairly early and in fact throughout  their  period of study in many cases as is the  case in our school system in canada  people are tested on the subjunctive in  french and things of that nature  or on their tenses  again i have to say when i start  learning a language i'm not that  interested  uh i'm kind of aware that there are  issues  in the slavic languages with cases there  are issues with verbs of motion i read  the explanations i vaguely understand  what they're getting at  i'm vaguely conscious of it as i read  and listen in the early stages but i  wouldn't want to have to be tested on it  i wouldn't want to have to get it right  some people may want to do that so then  that's fine  but why should the  student who is learning something that's  of interest to him or her in other words  the language and where the ultimate goal  is to understand what is said  and to be able to communicate  why should that student have to dance to  the teacher's tune why should the  student have to  write tests on proverbs or on  you know  colors or  or subjunctive or whatever the teacher  chooses to test the student on to me  it's meaningless  and i've said it before i've seen the  products of english language instruction  excuse me french language instruction  the english school system in canada and  the results are abysmal  i posted my efforts in czech and some  person said that that i'm already better  in czech than many of the people  who study the language  maybe it has something to do with the  fact that i enjoy  my check i enjoy it so much that i  listen every day and i listen to things  of interest and i read things of  interest and i build up my vocabulary  and now  that i have all of that i'm starting to  focus more  on  the fine points of grammar so that my  usage  gets better but for the first 10 months  i wasn't speaking the language so as  long as i can understand more or less  and i say more or less because i'm quite  happy to half understand or 80 percent  understand  uh but i'm enjoying it  as long as that all is happening and i'm  gaining experience in the language well  isn't that really good enough now you're  going to say well yeah but you can't do  that in the classroom because you have  to have a curriculum and i say why do  you have to have this curriculum  can't they come up with some other test  you know uh  to try to of course the teachers want  some test that is objective you either  know the proverb or you don't know the  proverb you either uh put the right  tense down or you don't put the right  tense down so you've got something that  is measurable  it's a little more difficult to say this  person understands this person  communicates well it becomes more of a  subjective  measure  so there is a problem i admit there's a  problem but  the solution in other words trying to  force students to learn what the teacher  taught to learn things that the teacher  thought were interesting  things that the teachers thought were  interesting or to learn in the order  that the teacher wants the student to  learn  i think in many cases that discourages  the learner and delays  the  arriving at the ultimate goal which is  the ability to communicate and to  comprehend  so  whether  those thoughts of mine have any  relevance to the classroom or not i  don't know but i couldn't help  wanting to  you know talk about it when i saw this  message or this comment on my video from  riva saying that in her czech classes if  i understand correctly  the students are asked to  you know be able to reproduce  czech proverbs which struck me as  completely ridiculous  uh not that prophets are ridiculous but  insisting that students who may have no  interest in proverbs like me still have  to reproduce proverbs struck me as  ridiculous anyway thanks for listening  bye for now
 

